# [What a Merkle DAG is]($heading.id('what'))

A *Merkle tree* is a tree in which each node is named by the hash of its
contents, and those contents include the hashes of its children. The name of
the root therefore commits to everything below it. Git commits, Bitcoin
blocks and IPFS all use one. *DAG* (directed acyclic graph) is the general
form: since nodes are named by content, two parents can point at the same
child, and identical subtrees are stored once.

NomadFS uses three kinds of block, each starting with a
[tag byte]($link.page('learn/sha-256').ref('cids')):

| Tag | Block | Contains |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `0x00` | leaf | the raw bytes of one chunk |
| `0x01` | parent | a list of links: child CID (32 B), subtree size (8 B), name |
| `0x02` | directory | a list of entries: CID, size, kind (file or dir), mtime, name |

All integers are big-endian, and a block's CID is the SHA-256 of these bytes.

# [Files]($heading.id('files'))

[FastCDC]($link.page('learn/fastcdc')) cuts a file into chunks, and each chunk
becomes a leaf. A parent block then links up to 174 leaves, in order, each
link recording the number of bytes under it. A file with more than 174 chunks
(about 43 MiB at the 256 KiB average) gets another level of parents over the
first, and so on until one block is left on top. That block's CID is the
file's root. A file of one chunk is just that leaf, and an empty file is an
empty leaf.

[A directory block points at a file's root; the parent's link sizes take a read straight to the leaf it needs.](<$image.siteAsset('learn/merkle-dag.svg').alt('A directory block for Photos lists 2025/, a dir that points to another dir block, and beach.jpg, a 1 MiB file that points to its file root. The file root is a parent block whose four links carry subtree sizes 246784, 317440, 202752 and 281600 bytes, each pointing to a leaf: a tag byte 0x00 plus the bytes. readAt offset 600000: 600000 minus 246784 is 353216, minus 317440 is 35776, which is less than 202752, so the read goes to leaf 3. Only two blocks are loaded: the parent and leaf 3.')>)

# [Reading a range]($heading.id('readat'))

The sizes in the links mean a reader never scans the whole file. To read
from byte offset *p* (`tree.readAt`):

1. Load the root. If it is a leaf, copy from it.
2. If it is a parent, walk its links: while *p* is at least the link's size,
   subtract the size and move on. The first link where *p* is smaller is the
   subtree holding the byte.
3. Load that child and repeat.

A read costs one block per level of the tree plus the leaf, whatever the
size of the file: two blocks for anything under about 43 MiB. Seeking in a
film on another device fetches the parent (once, then it is cached) and the
chunk at the seek point, not everything before it.

# [Folders]($heading.id('folders'))

A directory is one block listing its entries. Each entry has a name, a kind
(file or dir), a size (the file's length, or the total under a subfolder), a
modification time and a CID: a file's root, or the directory block of a
subfolder. Entries are sorted by name, and names are checked: at most 255
bytes, no `/`, `\` or NUL, never `.` or `..`. A listing from a peer can
then be mapped onto a local filesystem without escaping it, and a given
directory has exactly one encoding, so one CID.

Browsing another device's share is a walk down this tree:

- `GET_SHARES` returns a directory block whose entries are the device's
  shares, each pointing at the share's top directory.
- Opening a folder is one `GET_BLOCK` for its directory block. Listing a
  folder of large videos costs the same as listing a folder of text files.
- Opening a file walks from its root as above.

Every block fetched on the way is [checked against its CID]($link.page('learn/sha-256').ref('verify'))
and cached, so the tree you walked once can be walked again offline.

# [Scans and changes]($heading.id('scans'))

A shared folder is scanned into this tree when it is shared and again every
30 seconds. A file whose size and modification time match the last scan is
not read again; its CID is reused. When something changes, only the blocks
on the path from that file up to the top are new: its leaves (only the
chunks that changed), its parents, and the directory blocks above it. The
share's top CID changes, and peers see the new tree the next time they list
it.

# [The filestore: shares are not copied]($heading.id('filestore'))

Copying every shared file into the block store would double the disk space
used. For shared folders NomadFS doesn't store leaves. While scanning, it
records where each leaf's bytes are, as *(file, offset, length)*, and stores
only the parent and directory blocks, which are small.

When a peer asks for a leaf, the block store finds no file for it and asks
the filestore. The filestore reads those bytes from the original file and
hashes them again. If the file has changed since the scan, the hash no longer
matches and the block is reported as missing, never sent wrong. The next scan
records the new content.

Files in the [swarm folder]($link.page('learn/swarm-crdt')) are different:
they belong to the swarm rather than to a folder on one device, so their
blocks are stored in full in the block store of the device that added them.

Source: `src/storage/dag/codec.zig`, `src/storage/dag/builder.zig`,
`src/fs/tree.zig`, `src/fs/scan.zig`, `src/fs/filestore.zig`.
